  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe aim to minimize the AC1 upper bound by leveraging an iterative optimization method that combines linear programming to find descent directions with convex combinations for sequence updates. The core idea is to maintain the current sequence and improve its AC1 bound by finding directions that reduce the maximum autocorrelation value relative to the sum of elements squared. This approach builds upon the previous method but introduces a more robust initialization strategy and an improved polishing step to escape local minima. The method is theoretically sound because the Linear Program used to find descent directions ensures we stay in the feasible region while convex combinations help explore the neighborhood effectively.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe seek to minimize:\n    score = 2 * n * max(convolve(a, a, mode='full')) / (sum(a))^2\n\nSubject to:\n    - Each element a_i in the sequence is nonnegative and at most 1000.\n    - The sum of the sequence is at least 0.01.\n\nThe optimization uses the following formulation:\n    - Convolution: We compute the self-convolution using FFT for performance (especially for medium-length sequences).\n    - Linear Program: We solve the problem: maximize sum(g) s.t. convolution(f, g) <= M and g >= 0, where M is the current worst-case autocorrelation bound.\n    - Convex Combination Update: We update the sequence using the constraint that the new sequence also satisfies the feasibility conditions.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. **Initialization and validation**:\n   - Retrieve the parent sequence if available (height_sequence_1) and validate it.\n   - Prepare fallback sequences (uniform distribution) in case of normalization issues.\n   - Setup timing and budget management: allocate 60% to initializations, 30% to optimization iterations, and 10% for polishing.\n\n2. **Core Optimization Loop**:\n   - For each initialization candidate, solve the LP to obtain an update direction.\n   - Perform 10 descent iterations per initialization attempt.\n   - Use early termination if no improvement is observed or time exceeds budget.\n   - Maintain a separate best candidate outside the loop.\n\n3. **Polishing**:\n   - Apply local search moves (random scaling and coordinate adjustments) to refine the best candidate.\n   - Evaluate the polished candidate using the authoritative evaluator.\n\n4. **Termination and output**:\n   - After exhausting the budget, output the best candidate found.\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- **Initializations**: Use parent sequence (if available), uniform sequence, and cosine-based bumps.\n- **Update schedule**: Start with t=0.25 and adaptively vary t between t_min=0.015 and t_max=0.55 based on improvement.\n- **length**: Current implementation uses n=320. The evaluator allows any sequence length.\n- **Solver and termination**: Use 'highs' solver with PRESOLVE to reduce LP time. Stalling threshold is 100 iterations.\n\nComputational budget:\n- Time projection for full convolution: O(n log n)\n- Dense LP for large n (up to 640 constraints): ~0.5 seconds using 'highs'\n- Implemented inner loop with time checks to issue timely warnings.\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- For every new candidate before $deadline-15\\\", we validate using evaluate_sequence and filter candidates with minimum sum 0.01 and finite values.\n- The final sequence must be nonnegative and bounded by 1000 across all coefficients.\n- Use nested validation: only used the full evaluator on the best candidate after polishing.\n\nImplementation-details note:\n- The FFT convolution is used as needed, falling back to dense convolution for small n.\n- numpy, scipy, and CVXPY are used for linear programming and convolution operations.\n- All candidates are clipped to [0, 1000] after each update to maintain feasibility.\n\nAdditional hierarchical steps:\n1. Parent sequence download and basic checks.\n2. Fallback initialization for safety.\n3. Adam-like adaptive learning rate for updates.\n4. Complexity control by FFT-based convolution for medium-length sequences.",
